  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media carried continuous coverage of the September 5 tanker strikes under the framing of "American aggression against civilian energy infrastructure." IRNA published a Press TV clip on September 6 that identified the three struck vessels by name and described their crews as civilian workers; the same clip did not report the September 5 IRGC ballistic missile attack on US warships that preceded the US strikes. Tasnim ran the September 1 Araghchi-on-ceasefire position ("Iran holds the key to peace") without quoting Araghchi's August 19 demand for "a complete end to the war." → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>
      <p>Trump's September 9 "war ends after midterms" statement dominated Persian-language commentary. Press TV headlined "Trump admits political motive for Iran war" and broadcast segments from Iranian political analysts calling the admission evidence that the conflict had "no national security basis." Kayhan's editorial on September 10 was titled "The Empire Confesses Its Weakness" and called on the Supreme Leader to reject any ceasefire offer pending until after the US midterms. IRIB broadcast segments on the IAEA referral framing it as a US "pressure tool" rather than a nonproliferation enforcement action. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Araghchi's Pakistan channel continued to receive Tasnim coverage, described consistently as "diplomatic coordination on regional security" rather than Iran-US negotiation. A measurable internal framing gap emerged on September 11: IRNA and Tasnim carried different versions of the same Araghchi statement about Hormuz conditions, with IRNA using "ceasefire conditions" and Tasnim using "halt to aggression" as the operative phrase. The divergence in terminology between state media outlets, without any official correction, is a recurring pattern since August 15. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

